# Editor save publication independent negative review

Seven actual-client cases against immutable accepted126 produced four passing controls and three failing expectations. The source-bound success API invokes the real Editor Save handler, captures exact committed bytes, then holds only its response. No original engine, browser, host or benchmark ran.

P1: typing after the captured write commits produces a newer draft. The late response overwrites the document item's content with the captured request text, removes the dirty marker and reports zero diff while the textarea temporarily still shows the newer text. Selecting B then A drops the newer draft. The original disk commit is correct and remains distinct from this in-memory loss.

P2: a late success mutates tabs/status after disposal; a synthetic held failure changes the disposed status via the generic run() catch. The synthetic failure case performs no handle Save or disk commit. Exact raw observations preserve both boundaries.

No-typing, new-draft, selection and confirmed-close controls passed their content/active-tab requirements. Closing did not reopen the tab. This finite element model does not establish browser/workbench parity, complete process behavior or filesystem race safety. The source126 before/after proof and 127 inert snapshots remain exact.

A later correction must use a new stage after this negative epoch is frozen; no accepted126, previous review or production bytes were changed.
